  "usermeta": {
    "title": "Where Do Recognised Surfing Reserves Cluster?",
    "annotation": "The 150°–152°E, 34°–32°S cell contains six reserves (32% of Australia's 19 listed reserves), including Sydney and Central Coast localities.",
    "analysis": "Using the same 19 National Surfing Reserves as Chart 1, this map groups locations into fixed 2° longitude × 2° latitude cells. Ten cells contain at least one reserve. The strongest cluster is along the NSW coast between 150°–152°E and 34°–32°S, with six listings (32% of the national register). A second cell around the NSW–Queensland border contains three listings: Angourie, Lennox Head and Gold Coast. Margaret River and Yallingup occupy the same southwest WA cell, while more isolated recognised destinations such as Kalbarri, Point Sinclair, Daly Head, Phillip Island and Noosa occupy separate cells. Each cell uses the same angular dimensions, but not exactly the same ground area or coastline length; some cells cross state borders or extend over the sea. Counts therefore show geographic clustering of official listings, not surfing quality, visitor numbers or reserves per kilometre of surfable coast.",
    "source": {
      "label": "National Surfing Reserves — official register (19 localities; calculated 2° grid)",
      "url": "https://surfingreserves.org.au/national-surfing-reserves/"
    }
  },
